    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0644" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v16/pg622"/>

                <head>322. Telegram From the Embassy in Greece to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="0" xml:id="d322-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Athens</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-12-02T15:00:00+02:00">December 2, 1961,
                            3 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>699. Paris for USRO, Stoessel and McGuire. Rome for Rood. Main Greek effort in
                    connection Stikker visit<anchor n="1" xml:id="d322-f2"/> was to emphasize
                    crucial nature Greek need for continuing external aid order support its military
                    effort. Pointed to low per capita income, import-export imbalance. Estimated aid
                    of $35 million more aid required annually beyond present levels.</p>
                <p>Greeks pushed idea of NATO multilateral special fund to make up deficiencies
                    national military budgets. Stikker responded cautiously. Foreign Office NATO
                    Director reports Averoff intends approach Secretary in Paris to solicit his
                    support multilateral fund idea, and that Averoff will push for it at NATO
                    meeting.</p>
                <p>Airgram follows.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Briggs</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d322-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d322-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 375/12–261. Confidential; Priority.
                    Repeated to Paris Topol, Rome, and Ankara.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d322-f2" n="1" xml:id="d322-f2-content">
                <p>NATO Secretary General Dirk Stikker visited Athens December 2 for discussions
                    relating to NATO’s scheduled spring Ministerial Meeting. During his visit, the
                    Greek Government outlined its case for further military aid. The Embassy
                    reported on the Stikker visit in airgram A–296, December 2. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
